repository-code: >-
  https://github.com/rakshit-737/proactive-feasibility-scheduler
url: >-
  https://github.com/rakshit-737/proactive-feasibility-scheduler
abstract: >-
  An evaluation study of proactive GPU-cluster job scheduling driven by a
  machine-learning wait-time predictor, reporting a negative result: used as a
  queue-ordering score, such a model is structurally degenerate. At any dispatch
  instant all queued jobs share the cluster state, so every per-job feature in
  the standard feature set is a deterministic function of the job's requested
  size — the learned score therefore cannot distinguish two co-queued jobs by
  anything else. This is verified over 25,306 real dispatch instants with zero
  counterexamples, and an ML-free sort by requested size is shown statistically
  equivalent (paired TOST) to the full pipeline. The repository contains a
  30-phase research pipeline: discrete-time cluster simulation, wait-time
  regression modelling, a 14-scheduler synthetic benchmark and a trace-driven
  benchmark replaying Parallel Workloads Archive traces (LANL CM-5, SDSC SP2)
  with their real user runtime estimates, canonical EASY and conservative
  backfill, preemptive SRPT, HRRN, ablation and SHAP explainability, fairness
  and starvation analysis with a tunable fairness budget, uncertainty-aware
  scheduling, scaling and concept-drift studies, ROI analysis, and a one-command
  reproducibility kit.
keywords:
  - job scheduling
  - GPU cluster
  - high-performance computing
  - machine learning
  - wait-time prediction
  - backfilling
  - reproducible research
  - negative results
  - evaluation methodology
  - queue optimization
